      "answer": "itertools.groupby returns consecutive keys and groups from the iterable, so it groups consecutive runs, not equal keys anywhere in the input. [S1](<https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html>)\ngroupby generates a break or new group every time the value of the key function changes. [S1](<https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html>)\nTo collect all equal keys together, the iterable generally needs to already be sorted on the same key function. [S1](<https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html>)\nThe documentation states this behavior differs from SQL GROUP BY, which aggregates common elements regardless of input order. [S1](<https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html>)",
